  The blueprint-execution procedure. Rendered by st-execute-blueprint and by
  st-full-workflow's Step 3. Call sites supply: heading, heading_child, the seven
  step numbers (step_resolve through step_post), and the two trailing step numbers
  that post-execution-archive already names (summary_step, archive_step).

  "Locate the strikethroo root" is deliberately absent, for the same reason it is
  absent from procedure-generate-tasks: st-execute-blueprint owns it as its own step
  1, while st-full-workflow's Step 3 inherits the root from Step 1. That is why the
  two call sites number 2-8 and 1-7, and why every step number is a hash arg rather
  than a literal. step_resolve is also referenced in prose, so it stays in sync by
  construction.

  One slot, auto_generate_dispatch, carries the one genuine divergence: when tasks
  are missing, st-execute-blueprint dispatches across to the st-generate-tasks skill
  while st-full-workflow re-runs the procedure it already rendered as its own Step 2.
  Each call site supplies the slot as an {{#*inline}} block partial, so the branch is
  readable at the call site instead of being a conditional the agent has to evaluate
  here.

  Whitespace contract for this slot: it is one list item's text, not a block. The tag
  sits after the "- " marker on a bullet line and each {{#*inline}} definition is
  written on a single line carrying no newlines of its own, so the three-bullet list
  stays tight. procedure-create-plan's block slots use the opposite convention — tag
  alone on its own line, content carrying its own surrounding newlines — because an
  omitted block slot there has to collapse cleanly. This slot is never omitted; both
  call sites fill it, and an unfilled partial reference fails the build loudly.

  phase-execution-loop and post-execution-archive are called from here rather than
  from the templates. They keep their own heading / heading_parent / phase_step /
  summary_step / archive_step argument names; heading_child is the level one deeper
  than this procedure's own step headings, which is where both of them render their
  sub-headings. No arithmetic or string helper is registered, so both heading levels
  arrive as separate hash args.
--}}{{heading}} {{step_resolve}}. Resolve the plan

{{> plan-resolution}}

{{heading}} {{step_validate}}. Validate tasks and blueprint existence

Inspect the `taskCount` and `blueprintExists` values returned by the validation script.

{{heading}} {{step_generate}}. Auto-generate tasks and blueprint if missing

If `taskCount` is 0 or `blueprintExists` is `no`:

- Notify the user: "Tasks or execution blueprint not found. Generating tasks automatically..."
- {{> auto_generate_dispatch}}
- After generation completes, re-run `scripts/validate-plan-blueprint.cjs <plan-id> planFile` (and the other fields) to refresh the resolved paths and counts.

If generation still leaves the plan without tasks or a blueprint, stop and report failure. Do not attempt execution without a valid blueprint.

{{heading}} {{step_branch}}. Optionally create a feature branch

Run `scripts/create-feature-branch.cjs <plan-id>` once before phase execution. Branch creation is best-effort: when the script reports that it skipped creation (for example, not on `main`/`master`), continue on the current branch and do not retry or create a branch manually. Uncommitted or untracked changes are permitted only when every change is inside the repository-root `.ai/strikethroo` subtree, so a newly generated plan and tasks can remain uncommitted before execution. When the script exits with an error—including changes anywhere outside that subtree or an inability to inspect Git status on `main`/`master`—halt and report the error. Do not treat a skipped branch as a failure or spend effort working around a skip.

After the branch step, run `scripts/capture-base-commit.cjs <plan-id>` once. It records the commit the review gate diffs against. A `skipped` result is not a failure — continue execution and note that the review gate will skip. Only an `error` result halts.

{{heading}} {{step_context}}. Load project context and execution blueprint

Read these files, in order:

- `<root>/config/STRIKETHROO.md` — directory conventions and project context.
- The plan document at the path returned by step {{step_resolve}}.
- The plan's Execution Blueprint section — this defines the phase groupings and task dispatch order.
- `<root>/config/shared/verification-gate.md` and `<root>/config/shared/anti-rationalization.md` — apply in the phase loop below.

{{heading}} {{step_phases}}. Execute phases in order

{{> phase-execution-loop heading=heading_child phase_step=step_phases}}

Apply `<root>/config/shared/anti-rationalization.md` to this rationalization table:

| You catch yourself thinking… | The binding rule |
| --- | --- |
| "The subagent reported success, so the task is done." | A report is a claim, not evidence. Apply the verification gate before marking the phase complete. |
| "The tests probably pass." | "Probably" is a red flag. Run the proving command, read its output and exit code, then state the result. |
| "I'll verify later, after the next phase." | A phase is not complete until `POST_PHASE.md` succeeds against verified evidence. Verify now; do not advance on an unverified phase. |

